WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.426 --> 00:00:07.380
Biden's going to have a fairly
conventional view of America's

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.447
predominance in the world.

00:00:09.447 --> 00:00:11.610
China's been able to
gain a lot of ground

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:16.041
internationally by playing
off the US against Europe.

00:00:16.041 --> 00:00:18.150
One of the most
important differences

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.507
between Biden and Trump is
Biden does believe in diplomacy.

00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:24.060
China has shown
itself to be highly

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.800
pragmatic in terms of its
foreign relations with the US.

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:35.070
The US election was a month ago.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.310
The legal challenges are
all over, bar the tweeting,

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:42.720
so a presidential transition
is now fully underway.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.750
For president-elect
Biden, how he manages

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.780
the relationship with
China is likely to shape

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.950
his presidency and a big
part of how the world works.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.890
Ed, you've written a
lot about the impact

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.120
the Trump presidency has had on
the US's standing in the world,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.340
and that his reframing of the
west's attitude towards China

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.140
is regarded, in the US, as one
of his biggest achievements.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.140
So what would you say
are the key topics that

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.410
are going to define the US-China
relationship in the next four

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.137
years?

00:01:18.137 --> 00:01:19.830
So the most surprising
thing, I think,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.410
is just the element
of continuity here.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.200
As you say, Trump
has kind of branded

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.430
a new bipartisan
attitude towards China,

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.580
which sees it not as a
potential partner any longer,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.360
but which sees it as a
threat, a strategic rival,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.760
and that includes Democrats.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.900
There was, in my
view, less Sinophobia

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.540
in the presidential election
than you might have expected,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.600
but there was still quite
a lot, and it pushed Biden

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.060
quite sharply to
the right in terms

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.910
of where he used to be
as vice-president, a much

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.260
more hawkish voice on China.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:01.080
So I would expect the Biden
administration to pick up

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.110
from Trump in terms of
the hawkishness on trade

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.330
and on the deficit and
on intellectual property.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:12.390
I would expect there to be
considerable attempt to shore

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.170
up America's military
presence in the region,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.100
to continue that arms race,
if you like, with China,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.890
but also - and here's the
real departure from Trump -

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.780
to approach China with
allies, with partners,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.990
not through an
'America First' prism,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.640
but through multilateral means.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.350
One of the most
important differences

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:42.340
between Biden and Trump is
Biden does believe in diplomacy.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.110
He does believe in
working with allies.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:47.110
He does believe in what I
call the "constant gardening"

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:50.550
of talking to people and
working in concert with them.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.892
And he believes in the
multilateral system,

00:02:52.892 --> 00:02:54.350
and, this means a
couple of things.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.190
First, that he's
going to try and work

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.710
with Europe, and
with America's allies

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.040
and partners in the
Asia-Pacific region

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.100
to develop a common approach
to China on a range of issues.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.540
But he's also going to, perhaps
a bit more controversially,

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:15.820
and really in stark contrast
to Trump, and even to Obama,

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.920
is to set up a conference
of democracies.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.570
In other words, a kind
of ideological sort

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:26.680
of rivalry to China's
authoritarian model,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.740
a conscious sort of
ideological opposition

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.820
there that has been lacking
in US foreign policy, at least

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.690
since the Iraq war.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.550
Now, this common
approach, whether it's

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:43.180
a la carte minilateralism with
allies who want to join in,

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.540
or more conventional
multilateralism,

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.050
presumably, James,
is not something

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:54.700
that Xi Jinping and the China
leadership wants to see.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.940
It's really China's
worst nightmare.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:02.110
For the last 40 years of China's
phase of reform and opening

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.300
up to the outside
world, China's been

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:08.380
able to gain a lot of ground
internationally by playing off

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:13.600
the US against Europe
and really winning

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.550
some concessions in the process.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.320
If the vision of the future
is the US and Europe working

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.380
together to constrain
China in some way,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.350
that really closes
off a lot of doors

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.270
to the Chinese leadership.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.460
But I think there is
a valid question as to

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:36.220
whether or not the US and the
EU can actually pull this off.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.020
There's an awful
lot of co-ordination

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.570
that would be required
to actually achieve

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.900
this type of constraining
attitude towards China,

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.880
and I think if we
look at the cards

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.020
that China has in
its hand, you quickly

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.090
see how difficult it could be.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.630
Obviously the main one
is commerce and trade.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:59.230
China's the biggest trading
country in the world,

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:03.520
has a huge surplus with the US,
has a big surplus with the EU.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.740
There are all kinds
of trade levers

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.840
and other commercial
levers that the Chinese

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.040
can pull to frustrate and
prevent the US and the EU

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.150
working together against China.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.310
Biden will have the same
sort of overall view

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.230
that the commercial
relationship with China

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.800
is of transcending
importance that Trump has,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.250
but it will be a very different,
very different approach

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:33.790
to how he handles it.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:37.210
Trump saw it as in very
much 20th-century terms,

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:41.320
as where the bilateral trade
deficit was distributed

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.560
on aluminium or steel or
whatever it might have been,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.330
or soybeans.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:47.890
Biden won't be
obsessed with that.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:53.020
His team are looking more at
the strategic investment-driven

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:58.090
high-tech stuff,
particularly in AI and 5G,

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.670
and the strategic
advantages, as well

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:04.840
as the commercial
advantages, that China is

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.520
gaining on the United States.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.460
And I don't think they see the
Trump administration as having

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.410
really addressed any of that.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:13.870
So they will share
one thing, which

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:18.040
is China is a grand
commercial strategic threat,

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:23.170
but at that point, tactically,
they - Biden and Trump are

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.045
in different universes.

00:06:25.045 --> 00:06:27.730
From a Chinese perspective,
they look at the trade war

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:30.940
that Trump launched
in 2018, and I

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.188
think they feel that it
hasn't gone that badly.

00:06:35.188 --> 00:06:36.730
For instance, in
the last few months,

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.670
the Chinese trade
surplus with the US

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.160
has been rising
very sharply indeed.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.070
I think a lot of observers in
this part of the world, when

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.260
they think about
the trade war, they

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.260
think that China may have won.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.650
But the points that you
mention, the sanctions

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:57.280
by the US government on
key Chinese technologies,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.700
particularly in areas such as
artificial intelligence and 5G

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:04.840
telecoms, and sanctions directed
at particular Chinese companies

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.810
such as Huawei, I
think in those areas

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:11.140
China remains very exposed,
and those sanctions

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.870
have been very painful for
particular Chinese companies.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.200
So while the
leadership in Beijing

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.140
would be wanting those
to be eased or lifted,

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.360
I think they're
realistic in knowing

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.930
that progress on that
front is pretty unlikely.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:30.130
But beyond trade and this type
of commercial relationship, Ed,

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:34.660
let me ask you, how do you
see a President Biden dealing

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:39.100
with the geostrategic rivalry
that's come to characterise

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.780
the US-China relationship?

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:48.800
The interesting thing,
James, is that Biden's

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.710
going to have a fairly
conventional view of America's

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.620
predominance in the
world and the need

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.190
to maintain its hegemony
in the Asia-Pacific.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.770
So there'll still be
patrols of US naval

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:07.250
- freedom of navigation
patrols in the South China Sea.

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:10.610
There will still be a strong
treaty alliance with Japan.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.740
There will still be
military exercises, probably

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.130
more emphasised with
the quad of the US,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.230
Japan, Australia, and India.

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:20.810
It'll be referred
to as Indo-Pacific.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:25.880
So that conventional role
that America has always had,

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:29.780
and which Obama stepped up
with a pivot to Asia, Biden

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:33.240
will pick up on,
update, and strengthen.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:35.630
There's not going to be
any radical departure

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:38.780
there from the standard
American administration.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:41.090
Very radical departure,
though, from Trump

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:45.020
because there will be strong
emphasis on Hong Kong's

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.590
freedoms, or diminishing
freedoms on the Xinjiang

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:50.060
situation.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:52.970
They'll probably meet the
Dalai Lama at some point,

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:56.000
so that that's something
that China will not like,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.430
and with which it
will be very familiar.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:01.400
On the other hand, Biden
wants to work with China.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:05.330
He wants to reach out and
develop common positions

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.880
on issues like climate change.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.880
Without China, very little
can be accomplished.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.090
With China, you can get
dramatic global progress,

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:19.130
and the appointment of John
Kerry as Biden's climate envoy

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:23.720
is a signal that Biden doesn't
just want rivalry with China,

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.400
he also wants friendship.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.650
You could call it a sort
of 'frenemy' situation.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:32.840
So my question to you,
James, is, can China

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.520
do this sort of
walking and chewing

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:38.270
gum of being a rival and a
co-operator at the same time?

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.235
Would it... will that work?

00:09:40.235 --> 00:09:43.520
Yes, I think walking and
chewing gum for China

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.520
is situation normal in
international relations.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:49.280
I mean, I think for
probably a decade or more

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:52.850
before the Trump
presidency began,

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:57.410
China became used to balancing
rivalry on the one hand

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.890
and co-operation with
the US on the other.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:03.620
So I really don't think
that this will be a stretch.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.870
Of course, when
the US criticises

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:08.180
China on human rights
that will annoy Beijing

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:11.210
and there will be some
kind of a backlash.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.100
Of course there'll
be other irritations,

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.830
many other irritations
in the relationship,

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.230
but China has shown
itself to be highly

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:23.420
pragmatic in terms of its
foreign relations with the US.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.120
However, this time
around I think

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.850
there is one area
that is significantly

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:32.900
different from pre-the
Trump presidency,

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:36.770
and that is the situation and
the tensions around Taiwan.

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.680
Taiwan is regarded by China
as an inalienable part

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.260
of its territory,
but more and more is

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:46.430
showing self-determination under
the encouragement of the US.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:50.660
If tensions over Taiwan start
to really spiral then we

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:55.430
are genuinely in new territory,
and very scary territory,

00:10:55.430 --> 00:11:00.280
I think, for the whole region
and for the rest of the world.

